;; cps-call-arm-ignores-abi-specialization, face 1: the SILENT WRONG ANSWER.
;;
;; A CPS-lowered call must pick the ABI specialization whose RESULT type matches
;; this call site, not merely one whose argument types do.
;; find_mono_clone_for_call matched on argument C types alone, which identifies
;; nothing for a generic whose type variable reaches only the RESULT: `bt-scope
;; [A] [^fat body : (fn [] A)] : A` takes the erased int64 fat-thunk handle for
;; EVERY A, so every instantiation presents identical args.
;;
;; With A = int and A = a 2-int record in one program, the `/* cps->cps */` arm
;; handed the int site the RECORD's spec.  It then read the returned box POINTER
;; as its int and printed an address -- on default flags, through a stdlib
;; bracket.  So the number below has to be READ, not merely built: this compiles
;; perfectly and prints garbage.  Face 2, which is a build failure instead, is
;; tests/fixtures/cps-spec-erased-base-fallback-typechecks.
;;
;; Drop either defn and the other printed correctly even before the fix -- the
;; defect needs both instantiations to coexist -- and the two are
;; order-independent, so putting s-rec first does not move it.
;;
;; Green before the fix only because every bt-scope site in the tree returned
;; int, bool or void: all carrier-transparent, so base and specialization agree
;; and the mis-selection is invisible.  This is the first fixture that is not.

(load "stdlib/trail.tur")

(defdata SPair (SIP :int :int))

(defn s-int [n : int] : int   (bt-scope (fn [] (+ n 20))))
(defn s-rec [n : int] : SPair (bt-scope (fn [] (SIP n 20))))

(defn main [] : int
  (println (s-int 8))                            ;; 28 -- was an address
  (match (s-rec 8) (SIP a b) (println (+ a b)))   ;; 28
  0)
